WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.810
Over the past 30-plus years, Joel and Ethan
Coen have become one of the most prominent

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:07.020
filmmaking duos in the history of cinema.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:12.100
While it's all but impossible to find a genuine
black mark among their work, Coen Bros. movies

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.580
are not all created equal.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.800
While the Coen brothers' remake of the 1955
Brit-crime flick is often gorgeous to look

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.480
at, nothing else really works in The Ladykillers.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.040
The comedy falls flat.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.930
The moral quandaries are simplistic at best
and pandering at worst.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.980
Tom Hanks' turn as a would-be Southern gentleman
is particularly distracting.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.600
"Now I wanna know what's going on."

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.260
"Oh indeed, indeed, the thirst for knowledge
is a very commendable thing."

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.940
There's not much else to say about The Ladykillers
except that it's a rare, genuine misfire for

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:43.940
the talented duo.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.720
For those who don't recall this occasionally
hilarious crime picture, Intolerable Cruelty

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.730
stars George Clooney as a divorce lawyer and
Catherine Zeta-Jones as a gold-digger about

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.790
to dupe a billionaire oil baron.

00:00:55.790 --> 00:01:00.260
Sadly, despite palpable chemistry between
Clooney and Zeta-Jones, the film misses the

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:00.760
mark.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.380
Still, Intolerable Cruelty does feature one
of George Clooney's all-time greatest line

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:06.000
readings

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.540
"You fascinate me."

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.060
Ultimately,the film is bogged down by labored
plotting, thinly-drawn side characters, and

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.660
a third act that twists one too many times
for the film to rank among the Coens' best.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:28.140
Set on the backlots of a film studio in the
1950s, Hail, Caesar! saw the Coens making

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.930
a Hollywood fable about the studio system
of old.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.250
Told through the eyes of a "studio fixer,"
the movie dabbles in just about every film

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.560
genre you can imagine from crime to farce
and back again.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:43.360
Unfortunately, there's just a bit too much
going on for any one storyline to resonate.

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:47.100
That ultimately leaves Hail, Caesar! one of
the brothers' weaker works, although that

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.600
still makes it better than most movies that
get released.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:50.500
"Would that…

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.500
Would that it twere…"

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:52.500
"Would that it twere so simple."

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.970
"Would that it twere so simple."

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.050
"Would that it twere so simple."

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.820
"Would that it twere…

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.940
Would that it TWERE so simple."

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.660
Burn After Reading features a screenplay that
somehow ranks among both the darkest and funniest

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:09.019
the Coens have ever written, not to mention
stellar performances from George Clooney,

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:14.540
Frances McDormand, John Malkovich, Tilda Swinton,
and a marvelously over-the-top Brad Pitt.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:15.940
"The guy?"

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:18.220
"The guy!

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:18.960
The secret guy!"

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:21.700
"So is he high up?"

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:23.620
"Uh, I dunno if he's high up.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:24.120
Probably."

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:29.400
To be honest, if Burn After Reading had been
released anywhere else in the Coens' filmography,

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.180
it would likely rank higher on this list.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.920
Unfortunately, the film was released a year
after No Country for Old Men, and while Burn

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:40.109
is occasionally brilliant, it still comes
across a bit shallow in the shadow of such

00:02:40.109 --> 00:02:41.100
lofty fare.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.719
With its hard-to-love characters, its dialogue
that verges on annoying, its absurdist plot,

00:02:46.719 --> 00:02:50.879
and a twist of the fantastical that never
quite feels natural, The Hudsucker Proxy is

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.300
undoubtedly one of the most maligned and forgotten
films in the brothers' filmography.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.860
That's a shame, because a closer look reveals
well-drawn characters, a giddily ridiculous

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.100
plot, and clever, fast-paced dialogue.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.420
"Hiya chief, just the person I wanted to apologize
to."

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:05.720
"That's seven minutes."

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.600
"Yeah, I was all wet about your idea, man.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.960
Well, thanks for being so generous."

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:16.680
In short, The Hudsucker Proxy might be the
most misunderstood and underrated film the

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:17.680
Coens have made to date.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.460
It took some serious confidence to tackle
a remake of True Grit, a film held in high

00:03:22.469 --> 00:03:25.139
esteem by film fans and novices alike.

00:03:25.139 --> 00:03:29.889
The Coens wisely didn't try to reinvent the
wheel, allowing the Western narrative to unfold

00:03:29.889 --> 00:03:32.499
in pretty much the same fashion as the original.

00:03:32.499 --> 00:03:36.799
Of course, they also dressed the story up
with their own narrative and stylistic flourishes,

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:41.790
and pulled brilliant performances from Jeff
Bridges, Matt Damon, and then-newcomer Hailee

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:42.280
Steinfeld.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.180
Altogether, they just didn't make a great
remake of a classic film, they made one of

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:47.700
the best remakes of all time.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.980
If The Hudsucker Proxy is the most misunderstood
film in the Coens' body of work, it's only

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:57.360
because The Man Who Wasn't There is a film
all but beyond rational comprehension.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.440
Billy Bob Thornton plays a dispirited, sub-verbose
barber who thinks his ship has come in when

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:06.240
a stranger offers a chance to invest in a
new fad called "dry cleaning."

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.500
"Me, I don't talk much.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:15.280
I just cut the hair."

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:21.190
From that unusual setup, the brothers spin
one of the weirdest stories they've ever concocted,

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:25.030
and deliver a wholly original neo-noir thriller
fit for the modern age.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:28.820
It's an utterly unique film, even for the
idiosyncratic filmmakers.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:33.260
If their staggering body of work has taught
us anything, it's that the Coen Brothers have

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:35.710
no shortage of original stories to tell.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:40.420
For proof, look no further than The Ballad
of Buster Scruggs, a sprawling, Western anthology

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:43.550
film composed of six disparate tales of the
west.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:47.570
Some are funny, others dramatic, and a couple
are downright heartbreaking.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:52.020
In true Coen fashion, those elements are often
at play within the same story, making The

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:57.400
Ballad of Buster Scruggs a giddy and galvanizing
cinematic experience, even if it isn't quite

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.660
coherent enough to rank among their very best.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:01.240
"We all love about ourselves.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.960
So long as the people in the stories are us
but not us.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.020
Not us in the end especially."

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.680
Set in a bustling, prohibition-era metropolis,
Miller's Crossing follows the chief advisor

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.540
of a mob kingpin as they try to navigate the
shifting tides of a particularly nasty turf

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:19.040
war.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.880
Of those tides, we'll say that they never
lead quite where you'd expect, that the stakes

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:26.200
are high, and that saying any more would spoil
the fun of watching the film.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:30.460
What we can say is that of the Coens produced
writing credits, Miller's Crossing undeniably

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:32.160
stands as some of their finest work.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.900
It's a harrowing mistaken identity crime thriller
about an average dude trying to replace his

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:38.910
rug.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:42.750
It's a campy comedy classic about a fish out
of water who gets in way over his head in

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.170
the seedy underground of the San Fernando
Valley.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:49.730
It's a character study about an aging stoner
whose ambition in life is to smoke, drink

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:53.450
White Russians, and occasionally hit the bowling
alley for league play.

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:57.630
It's also one of the greatest cult films ever
made and one of the most quotable scripts

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:58.630
ever produced.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:02.880
Look, there's really not much we can say about
The Big Lebowski that hasn't been said already.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:07.720
You either abide The Big Lebowski as the masterfully
envisioned madcap stoner epic masterpiece

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.000
that it is or, well, you're just wrong.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:16.060
"Yeah, well, y'know, that's just like… your
opinion, man."

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:20.760
Like crime and criminals, religion has often
found its way into the Coens' films.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:25.600
In their caustic farce A Serious Man, the
brothers deliver a complicated religious parable

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:29.800
about a Jewish physics professor searching
for meaning as his life falls apart around

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:30.300
him.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:34.620
Watching a man's entire life crumble hardly
sounds like the formula for great comedy.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.740
But even in the film's heaviest moments, the
Coens add just enough levity to keep it from

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.010
becoming a blunt trauma sort of drama.

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:45.590
If you grew up in the Jewish faith, the film,
with its winking in-jokes and scores of Judaic

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:47.840
details, will hit extremely close to home.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:49.680
O Brother, Where Art Thou?

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.820
Is a depression-era tale about penniless escaped
convicts perilously traversing the American

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:57.000
South in search of a fabled treasure.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.540
The Coens claim Homer's epic poem The Odyssey
as the film's source material, though they

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:04.050
also claim to have never read the poem itself.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:09.601
That's okay, because the batty, beautiful,
alterna-musical they crafted is a timeless,

00:07:09.601 --> 00:07:11.330
hilarious southern fried epic in its own right.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:16.520
It finally brought the Coens' off-kilter sensibilities
to the mainstream, and its bluegrassy soundtrack

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.660
became a legit cultural phenomenon.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.000
A tragic tale of a kidnapping scheme gone
unimaginably wrong against the icy backdrops

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.460
of North Dakota and Minnesota, Fargo features
everything you could possibly want from a

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:30.020
Coen brothers film.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:30.700
Don't argue with us.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:36.020
"I, I'm not eh, I'm not arguing here!

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:37.120
I'm cooperating.

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:38.880
And there's…

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:39.920
We're doing all we can."

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:44.760
While Fargo is one of the Coens' more serious
cinematic ventures, it still features their

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:49.580
signature dark humor, vibrant language, colorful
characters, and of course, brutal outbursts

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:50.640
of violence.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:55.380
It all comes together to make Fargo one of
the most uniquely Coenesque movies ever made.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:00.780
Set against the barren, dusty vistas of a
desolate desert town, Raising Arizona tells

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:05.580
the story of a couple who, in the face of
fertility troubles, decide to kidnap a baby.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:10.120
Raising Arizona only gets crazier as that
improbable narrative unfolds… which maybe

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:11.630
raises a few questions.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:15.040
Is Raising Arizona a comedy of errors about
lovable kidnappers?

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:15.540
Yes.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:20.180
Is it a Reagan-era parable about trickle-down
economics and prison reform gone haywire?

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:21.000
Seems like it.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:25.560
Is Raising Arizona a radically idealistic
farce that heralded the arrival of a bold

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:26.660
new cinematic vision?

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:27.820
One hundred percent.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:31.880
It's crazy in all the best ways, and if you
don't believe that, you simply cannot call

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:33.320
yourself a Coen Bros. fan.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:37.959
As the story goes, while the brothers were
writing Miller's Crossing, they hit a creative

00:08:37.959 --> 00:08:40.509
wall so fierce they put the project on hold.

00:08:40.509 --> 00:08:45.259
In hopes of kickstarting their creative juices,
they conceptualized a new film centered around

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:48.160
a character created specifically for John
Turturro.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:53.280
From the creative void of writer's block,
Barton Fink, a wildly original, scathingly

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:56.140
satirical, pseudo-mystical masterpiece was
born.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:09:00.879
A masterpiece about, of all things, a writer
facing a devastating creative block, not to

00:09:00.879 --> 00:09:05.760
mention a full-fledged metaphysical crisis
of identity that may or may not directly involve

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:06.340
the devil.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:10.860
Simply put, Barton Fink is a haunting look
at the life of the creative mind the likes

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:14.820
of which moviegoers had never seen before
and possibly even since.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:20.140
Llewyn Davis is manipulative and self-centered,
but he's also an intensely likable guy who

00:09:20.149 --> 00:09:21.870
usually tries to do the right thing.

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:27.259
That messy mixture is what makes Inside Llewyn
Davis such a fascinating experiment in character.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:28.660
"I have the money, don't worry."

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.240
"With you I worry."

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:30.820
"Well you shouldn't."

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:31.960
"Yes I should.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.000
God knows you never do."

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:38.580
It helps that the character is played with
equal parts charm, swagger, and crippling

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.540
insecurity by Oscar Isaac in a star-making
turn.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:46.140
Watching Isaac portray such an identifiable,
beautifully flawed character is enough to

00:09:46.149 --> 00:09:48.259
make Inside Llewyn Davis worth a look.

00:09:48.259 --> 00:09:52.920
That the music, the settings, and the supporting
characters are crafted with equal skill and

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:56.839
care, and that's what makes Inside Llewyn
Davis one of the Coens' most memorable works

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:57.380
to date.

00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:02.060
While it's hard to believe that the Coens
made their feature debut over 30 years ago,

00:10:02.069 --> 00:10:05.700
it's harder still to believe just how much
they got right with that debut film.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:10.730
Make no mistake, Blood Simple is still every
bit the biting, brutal, exactingly scripted,

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:14.550
and exquisitely photographed noir-soaked thriller
it was upon its release.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:18.700
Its menacing, white-knuckle finale will leave
your palms sweaty as it unfolds before your

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:19.200
eyes.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.620
Blood Simple is one of the most staggeringly
assured debut films ever produced, and it

00:10:23.621 --> 00:10:25.509
remains one of the Coen Brothers' best.

00:10:25.509 --> 00:10:26.160
Period.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:31.600
Written by legendary scribe Cormac McCarthy
and possessed of stark Western themes and

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:37.819
bracing wit, No Country for Old Men seemed
tailor made for The Coens' peculiar sensibilities.

00:10:37.819 --> 00:10:42.170
Sensibilities aside, the Coens took the bones
of McCarthy's novel and ran with them, delivering

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:46.529
a high-minded art film dressed up as one of
the greatest crime/chase thrillers cinema

00:10:46.529 --> 00:10:47.240
has ever seen.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:52.160
Along the way, they pulled startlingly sparse
performances from Tommy Lee Jones and Josh

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:55.490
Brolin, and an iconically villainous turn
from Javier Bardem.

00:10:55.490 --> 00:11:00.119
In short, No Country stands as the crowning
achievement of Joel and Ethan Coen's career

00:11:00.119 --> 00:11:01.129
in film.

00:11:01.129 --> 00:11:02.420
"Is something wrong?"

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:03.200
"With what?"

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:03.860
"With anything?"

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:07.180
"Is that what you're asking me?

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:09.640
Is there something wrong with anything?"

